feat(hook): refuse a destructive git while an agent run holds work here (#278)

Measured: a `git reset --hard HEAD~1` in a shared checkout, dropping a throwaway
probe commit, discarded every tracked modification in the tree -- including one
subagent's finished but uncommitted work. Untracked files survived, tracked
edits did not, which is exactly what a hard reset does. Eight minutes of work
went, and nothing refused, warned or recorded it.

Agent work lives in the working tree until the SubagentStop capture commits it
(ADR-0046). Between the last edit and that capture it has no protection at all,
and the operator shares the tree.

WHERE IT SITS, which is the decision worth arguing. The obvious place is a git
hook, and git has none that would work: there is no pre-reset, no pre-checkout
and no pre-clean, and `post-checkout` runs after the tree has been rewritten.
The one abortable hook a `reset --hard` fires is `reference-transaction`, and it
is too late -- MEASURED in a scratch repository, a hook exiting non-zero on
`prepared` left `fatal: ref updates aborted by hook`, exit 128, HEAD where it
was, AND the uncommitted line already gone. git resets the index and the working
tree before it opens the ref transaction. A guard there would report a refusal
over a tree that had already lost the thing it was guarding, which is worse than
no guard because it reads as protection.

So the decision lives in scripts/hooks/git-tree-guard.sh and two callers can
reach it. `hook` is the harness's PreToolUse, which sees an agent's Bash calls
and is the only thing that can block one; the shim consults it, so that half
needs no operator action. `wrap` is a `git` earlier on PATH, which is the only
place that sees a HUMAN's terminal -- and the human is who the measured incident
was. Nothing here installs that; a file that inserts itself in front of git on
somebody's machine is not a change a script may make on its own.

WHAT IT DOES NOT COVER is in the file, at length, because a guard believed to
cover more than it does is the failure it exists to prevent: a human's terminal
until the wrapper is on PATH; an absolute path, a shell alias or an editor's own
git; a `git` inside a script or an `eval`, which is not in the string the hook
was handed; and any other harness.

WHAT IT REFUSES: reset --hard/--merge/--keep, checkout with a pathspec or -f,
restore unless it is --staged only, switch --discard-changes, clean -f. Tracked
and untracked are weighed separately, because the incident turned on exactly
that distinction: `clean -f` cannot destroy a tracked edit and `reset --hard`
cannot destroy an untracked file, so neither is refused over work it cannot
reach. `reset --soft`, `reset --mixed` and `git stash` are not refused at all.

WHO IS LIVE is asked of the ledger, not of the marker. A subagent killed with
its session fires no SubagentStop and leaves its marker behind forever (#260
measured five such runs Active for hours), so a guard trusting the marker would
wedge that tree permanently. `status: active` is live; when the ledger does not
answer, the marker's age against the reaper's grace decides (IP 6.7).

WHAT A RUN CLAIMS is what its own tool-call bodies record it writing, the same
source that bounds the capture in #261 -- a dirty path no live run wrote belongs
to whoever wrote it. And a live run whose body store cannot be read is refused
over rather than past: same rule as #284 one level out, because a run nobody can
ask is not a run that wrote nothing, and the costs are asymmetric. A wrong
refusal costs one environment variable; a wrong allow costs the work.

Two things were found by driving the installed gate rather than the selftest,
and both are in it now as cases. The harness reports the SESSION's cwd on every
event, so `cd <tree> && git reset --hard` was weighed against a tree it was
never going to touch and allowed -- `cd` is followed now. And a PreToolUse hook
is a child of the harness, not of the shell, so the exact line the refusal
printed arrived with the variable unset and was refused again; the override is
read out of the command as well as the environment.

Every failure allows. No python3, an unreadable runs directory, a git that will
not answer, a command that will not tokenise, a guard that is not installed:
the only thing worse than an unguarded reset is a shell that cannot run git.

Driven by OPS-090..094 in the new scripts/hooks/git-tree-guard-selftest.sh --
39 assertions against a real repository, with the real command attempted
through `wrap`, so a refusal is asserted as a file that still holds its line
rather than as an exit status. Against a stand-in that allows everything, which
is the repository as it stood, 20 of 33 were red and every one of them red on
merit: the tree came back without the agent's work in it. The `cd` case and the
override case were each red against the guard as it stood minutes earlier.
OPS-095 in scripts/hooks/shim-selftest.sh covers the shim reaching it: 9
assertions, all 9 red against the shim as it stood -- nothing blocked -- and
each one carries the block, so none of them can pass by allowing everything.
97 and 39 pass, 0 fail. Both files were driven under sh, bash, zsh and dash,
which refused byte for byte identically.

